  </div>
  <div class="chart-box">
    <h4>Monthly Order Volume</h4>
    <div class="chart-wrap"><canvas id="monthlyOrders"></canvas></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="callout">
  <strong>Trend read-out.</strong> 2023 revenue closed at {fmt_money(d['yearly'][0]['revenue'])} ({fmt_int(d['yearly'][0]['orders'])} orders); 2024 closed at {fmt_money(d['yearly'][1]['revenue'])} ({fmt_int(d['yearly'][1]['orders'])} orders). Month-to-month volatility is narrow — the business does not exhibit sharp seasonal spikes in this dataset, which is atypical for chocolate retail. Management should verify whether holiday-season signal is present in source systems (the fact table alone may smooth across promotional windows).
</div>

<!-- PRODUCT PERFORMANCE -->
<h2 class="section">Product &amp; Category Performance</h2>
<p class="lede">With {d['unique_products']} distinct SKUs in circulation, product-level contribution to revenue and profit varies meaningfully. The top 10 products below represent the revenue anchors of the catalog.</p>

<div class="caveat">
  <strong>Data scope note:</strong> The provided fact table contains product IDs but not brand, category, or cocoa-percentage attributes (the referenced <code>products.csv</code> dimension table was not supplied in this analysis input). Category-, brand-, and cocoa-% level breakdowns have therefore been omitted rather than fabricated. Attaching the product dimension would unlock those cuts.
</div>

<div class="chart-box">
  <h4>Top 10 Products by Revenue</h4>
  <div class="chart-wrap tall"><canvas id="topProducts"></canvas></div>
</div>

<div class="card">
  <h3>Top 10 Products — Detail</h3>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Product ID</th><th>Revenue</th><th>Profit</th><th>Margin</th><th>Units Sold</th><th>Orders</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
"""
for p in tp:
    html += f"      <tr><td>{p['product_id']}</td><td>{fmt_money2(p['revenue'])}</td><td>{fmt_money2(p['profit'])}</td><td>{fmt_pct(p['margin'])}</td><td>{fmt_int(p['units'])}</td><td>{fmt_int(p['orders'])}</td></tr>\n"

html += f"""    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

<div class="card">
  <h3>Bottom 5 Products by Revenue</h3>
  <p>The lowest-revenue SKUs below are candidates for range-review: whether to refresh packaging, reposition pricing, or phase out in favor of higher-rotation items.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Product ID</th><th>Revenue</th><th>Profit</th><th>Margin</th><th>Orders</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
"""
for p in d['bottom_products']:
    html += f"      <tr><td>{p['product_id']}</td><td>{fmt_money2(p['revenue'])}</td><td>{fmt_money2(p['profit'])}</td><td>{fmt_pct(p['margin'])}</td><td>{fmt_int(p['orders'])}</td></tr>\n"

html += f"""    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

<!-- STORE ANALYSIS -->
<h2 class="section">Store &amp; Geographic Performance</h2>
<p class="lede">{d['total_stores']} retail locations contributed to total revenue. The distribution below highlights the top and bottom performers — management's primary levers for either scaling winning-store playbooks or remediating underperformers.</p>

<div class="caveat">
  <strong>Data scope note:</strong> Store-level attributes (city, country, store type) from the <code>stores.csv</code> dimension were not provided with the analysis input. Geographic and store-type cuts are therefore unavailable in this report; only store-ID-level aggregates are shown. Attaching store dimension data would enable country, city, and store-type comparisons.
</div>

<div class="chart-box">
  <h4>Top 10 Stores by Revenue</h4>
  <div class="chart-wrap tall"><canvas id="topStores"></canvas></div>
</div>

<div class="grid-2">
  <div class="card">
    <h3>Top 10 Stores</h3>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Store</th><th>Revenue</th><th>Margin</th><th>Orders</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
"""
for s in ts:
    html += f"        <tr><td>{s['store_id']}</td><td>{fmt_money2(s['revenue'])}</td><td>{fmt_pct(s['margin'])}</td><td>{fmt_int(s['orders'])}</td></tr>\n"

html += f"""      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="card">
    <h3>Bottom 5 Stores</h3>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Store</th><th>Revenue</th><th>Margin</th><th>Orders</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
"""
for s in d['bottom_stores']:
    html += f"        <tr><td>{s['store_id']}</td><td>{fmt_money2(s['revenue'])}</td><td>{fmt_pct(s['margin'])}</td><td>{fmt_int(s['orders'])}</td></tr>\n"

top_store_rev = ts[0]['revenue']
bot_store_rev = d['bottom_stores'][0]['revenue']
html += f"""      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="callout">
  <strong>Spread between top and bottom is wide.</strong> Top store generates {fmt_money2(top_store_rev)} vs bottom-ranked at {fmt_money2(bot_store_rev)} — a {top_store_rev/bot_store_rev:.1f}× gap. A next step is to pair each bottom-quartile store with a top-quartile mentor store and audit assortment + staffing alignment.
</div>

<!-- DISCOUNT ANALYSIS -->
<h2 class="section">Discount Impact &amp; Profitability Analysis</h2>
<p class="lede">Discounts were applied on <strong>{fmt_pct(d['discount_util_rate'])}</strong> of transactions. The critical question for management: do discounts drive incremental value, or do they erode margin without producing offsetting volume?</p>

<div class="grid-2">
  <div class="chart-box">
    <h4>Revenue &amp; Profit by Discount Tier</h4>
    <div class="chart-wrap"><canvas id="discRev"></canvas></div>
  </div>
  <div class="chart-box">
    <h4>Profit Margin by Discount Tier</h4>
    <div class="chart-wrap"><canvas id="discMargin"></canvas></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="chart-box">
  <h4>Average Order Value by Discount Tier</h4>
  <div class="chart-wrap"><canvas id="discAOV"></canvas></div>
</div>

<div class="card">
  <h3>Discount Tier Detail</h3>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Discount Tier</th><th>Orders</th><th>Order Share</th><th>Revenue</th><th>Profit</th><th>Margin</th><th>Avg Order Value</th><th>Avg Qty</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
"""
for t in d['discount_tiers']:
    html += f"      <tr><td>{t['disc_tier']}</td><td>{fmt_int(t['orders'])}</td><td>{fmt_pct(t['order_share'])}</td><td>{fmt_money2(t['revenue'])}</td><td>{fmt_money2(t['profit'])}</td><td>{fmt_pct(t['margin'])}</td><td>{fmt_money2(t['avg_order_value'])}</td><td>{t['avg_qty']:.2f}</td></tr>\n"

# Critical discount insight
no_disc_aov = d['discount_tiers'][0]['avg_order_value']
deep_disc_aov = d['discount_tiers'][-1]['avg_order_value']
aov_erosion = (no_disc_aov - deep_disc_aov) / no_disc_aov * 100

html += f"""    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

<div class="callout">
  <strong>Key insight — discounts are eroding AOV without lifting volume.</strong>
  <ul class="insight-list">
    <li>Profit margins are essentially <strong>flat at ~40% across every discount tier</strong>. This means unit-cost structure is being preserved — but it also means the current pricing ladder is calibrated so that discounts do not gain share of higher-margin basket.</li>
    <li>Average order value drops from <strong>{fmt_money2(no_disc_aov)}</strong> (no discount) to <strong>{fmt_money2(deep_disc_aov)}</strong> (16–20%+ discount) — a <strong>{fmt_pct(aov_erosion)} AOV erosion</strong> for deeply discounted orders.</li>
    <li>Average quantity per transaction is <strong>~3.0 units across all tiers</strong> — discounts are <em>not</em> producing basket-expansion. Customers take the lower price on the same basket size.</li>
    <li><strong>Management recommendation:</strong> either (a) tighten discount governance to reserve deep discounts for genuine clearance/velocity problems, or (b) test "buy 3 get 1" / bundle mechanics designed explicitly to shift the quantity distribution upward, rather than flat percentage discounts that the data shows are non-incremental.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<!-- CUSTOMER INSIGHTS -->
<h2 class="section">Customer Behavior &amp; Loyalty Insights</h2>
<p class="lede">Of {fmt_int(d['unique_customers'])} distinct customers, the average buyer completed {d['avg_orders_per_cust']:.2f} orders for {fmt_money2(d['avg_revenue_per_cust'])} in lifetime revenue. Segmenting by purchase frequency reveals where concentration — and growth headroom — live.</p>

<div class="caveat">
